### name: op.in.one-value.exact-column.mariadb
--- note
SPEC §5.4: `x IN y` with a childless `y` IS `x EQL y`, and `("ab")` is parentheses around one value, not a one-item list. The scalar branch of IN used its own transcription of EQL's operand rule, which cast the literal beside a column declared exact, where EQL leaves the column plain against a text literal: the comparison became case-exact under the binary collation and the column's index could not serve it. It is now translated AS EQL; the `eql-twin` case beside each pins the same bytes.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"A": {"kind": "column", "column": "value", "type": "TEXT", "exact": true}}
--- source
A IN ("ab")
--- expect
(`value` = 'ab')
===

### name: op.in.one-value.exact-column.eql-twin.mariadb
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"A": {"kind": "column", "column": "value", "type": "TEXT", "exact": true}}
--- source
A EQL "ab"
--- expect
(`value` = 'ab')
===

### name: op.in.one-value.exact-column.two-values.mariadb
--- note
The list branch beside it: a literal list keeps the exact column plain as well, so one value and two read alike.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"A": {"kind": "column", "column": "value", "type": "TEXT", "exact": true}}
--- source
A IN ("a", "b")
--- expect
((`value` = 'a') OR (`value` = 'b'))
===

### name: op.in.one-value.plain-column.mariadb
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
T IN ("ab")
--- expect
(CAST(`name` AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_nopad_bin = CAST('ab' AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_nopad_bin)
===

### name: op.in.one-value.plain-column.eql-twin.mariadb
--- dialect
mariadb
